          "date": "2026-11-06 03:17:56.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "committer": {
          "name": "patrick bot",
          "email": "patrick-bot@mulberrytree.us",
          "date": "2026-11-06 03:17:56.000000000",
          "tz": 0
        },
        "subject": "LU-20076 tests: do not sleep before the checks in fail()",
        "message": "LU-20076 tests: do not sleep before the checks in fail()\n\nclients_up() sleeps for a second before its \"lfs df\". The\nsleep comes from fail_abort(), which once ran \"df\", allowed\nit to fail, slept and ran it again. When those calls became\nclients_up(), every caller got the sleep.\n\nfail() calls clients_up() twice: once to wake idle OSC\nconnections, then after wait_clients_import_ready() as the\nfinal check. Neither sleep is needed. The first call only\nhas to send the statfs, and the second follows a wait for the\nfailed facets' imports, which wait_clients_import_ready()\naccepts as FULL or IDLE. The second \"lfs df\" may be answered\nfrom the client's statfs cache (two seconds) instead of a new\nRPC, but a target that failed the first call is not cached,\nso the check still shows that each target answered. Call\nlfs_df_check() directly there. fail_abort() and the tests\nthat call clients_up() keep the sleep.\n\nThat is 2 s per fail(), on top of the sleep in\nfacet_failover() (SOFT mode) that this patch does not\ntouch. If all of the roughly 2 million failovers in 30 days\nwent through fail(), that is up to about 1100 hours of test\ntime. The command and timing figures of the earlier changes\nin the series do not include this one.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial testlist=replay-single,recovery-small\nTest-Parameters: trivial testlist=replay-dual,insanity\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5\nSigned-off-by: Patrick Farrell <patrick@thelustrecollective.com>\nChange-Id: If8e9c129bbd56b289700ab129e6dbb2721779fc1\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master",
      "description": "Edit commit message"
    }
